1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:07,880
Listen.

2
00:00:08,530 --> 00:00:09,050
Practice.

3
00:00:09,570 --> 00:00:09,910
Speak.

4
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:12,250
Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:14,050 --> 00:00:19,680
Ever hit send on an email only to immediately wonder if you chose the absolute best words?

6
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:20,300
Oh, definitely.

7
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:21,160
Happens all the time.

8
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:26,930
Or maybe you've stared at a blank screen sort of wrestling with how to be professional and genuinely effective.

9
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:28,960
It's a universal dilemma, really.

10
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:29,360
Yeah.

11
00:00:29,850 --> 00:00:33,700
In today's digital world, our emails are more than just messages, right?

12
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:37,050
They're often our first and sometimes only impression.

13
00:00:37,110 --> 00:00:37,950
That's spot on.

14
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:46,070
That's why today we're taking a deep dive into the, well, surprisingly powerful world of essential business email phrases.

15
00:00:46,790 --> 00:00:56,480
Our mission for this deep dive is basically to give you a shortcut, you know, to sounding polished, professional, and strategic in every single email you send.

16
00:00:56,520 --> 00:00:58,350
And it's not just about sounding good, is it?

17
00:00:58,390 --> 00:01:02,170
It's about being understood and often getting what you need.

18
00:01:02,410 --> 00:01:03,030
Exactly.

19
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:03,210
Exactly.

20
00:01:03,190 --> 00:01:07,780
We've pulled together insights from a pretty comprehensive essential business email phrases handbook.

21
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:15,630
And what's fascinating here is how like subtle shifts in language can dramatically shape perception and outcomes.

22
00:01:15,650 --> 00:01:15,990
Right.

23
00:01:16,010 --> 00:01:17,410
Little words, big impact.

24
00:01:17,670 --> 00:01:18,270
Yeah.

25
00:01:18,290 --> 00:01:20,470
We'll explore phrases for every interaction.

26
00:01:20,730 --> 00:01:20,830
Yeah.

27
00:01:20,850 --> 00:01:26,040
You know, from those crucial opening lines all the way to delicate negotiations.

28
00:01:26,060 --> 00:01:28,300
So you're not just informed, but actually equipped.

29
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:29,460
That's the goal.

30
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:32,000
Equipped to navigate your inbox like a pro.

31
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:36,670
Okay, think of this deep dive then as your advanced training for digital communication.

32
00:01:37,190 --> 00:01:44,420
We're going to distill key takeaways that go beyond the obvious, giving you practical tools and honestly some truly surprising insights.

33
00:01:44,580 --> 00:01:47,470
Make your messages more impactful and crucially.

34
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:50,310
Less prone to misinterpretation, definitely.

35
00:01:50,350 --> 00:01:52,410
Ready to upgrade your digital handshake?

36
00:01:52,550 --> 00:01:53,010
Let's do it.

37
00:01:52,990 --> 00:01:54,330
OK, let's kick things off.

38
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:58,520
We all know those first few lines of an email, they set the entire tone.

39
00:01:59,420 --> 00:02:04,870
But the real trick is hitting that sweet spot, showing courtesy while immediately getting to the point.

40
00:02:05,450 --> 00:02:08,550
How do you, you know, manage that delicate balance?

41
00:02:08,710 --> 00:02:10,520
Well, it's about psychological priming, really.

42
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:13,380
Take a classic like, hope this email finds you well.

43
00:02:13,580 --> 00:02:15,100
OK, yeah, everyone uses that one.

44
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:15,530
Right.

45
00:02:16,530 --> 00:02:27,220
On the surface, it's polite, but its true power lies in subtly building rapport, especially, say, after a period of silence or with someone you don't interact with daily.

46
00:02:27,620 --> 00:02:29,890
Ah, so it primes them for a positive exchange.

47
00:02:30,130 --> 00:02:30,550
Exactly.

48
00:02:31,110 --> 00:02:32,750
But here's the catch.

49
00:02:32,770 --> 00:02:34,350
Use it too often with the same person.

50
00:02:34,490 --> 00:02:36,090
It starts to feel a bit hollow.

51
00:02:36,380 --> 00:02:36,840
Precisely.

52
00:02:36,900 --> 00:02:40,420
It ironically starts to feel generic, even like algorithmic.

53
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:40,820
Yeah.

54
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:43,600
It erodes the very connection it aims to foster.

55
00:02:43,690 --> 00:02:45,430
That's a great point about overusing it.

56
00:02:45,510 --> 00:02:48,360
It can go from warm to almost robotic.

57
00:02:48,440 --> 00:02:48,640
Yeah.

58
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:53,530
Another one I often use, especially on a Monday, is I trust you had a pleasant weekend.

59
00:02:53,630 --> 00:02:55,590
Now that's a fantastic humanizing touch.

60
00:02:56,030 --> 00:02:57,010
It shows consideration.

61
00:02:57,120 --> 00:03:01,310
And for Monday mornings or maybe after a holiday, it builds genuine rapport.

62
00:03:01,370 --> 00:03:01,670
Right.

63
00:03:01,750 --> 00:03:04,220
It signals you see them as more than just a task doer.

64
00:03:04,240 --> 00:03:04,520
It does.

65
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:18,260
Now, for those particularly sensitive situations, especially during, say, difficult global events or company crises, a phrase like, I hope this message reaches you in good spirits despite the challenging circumstances, that one is incredibly powerful.

66
00:03:18,410 --> 00:03:20,180
Wow, that's truly empathetic.

67
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:25,680
It acknowledges a shared reality without needing to, you know, delve into specifics.

68
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:26,220
Exactly.

69
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:29,750
It's critical for maintaining professionalism and sensitivity.

70
00:03:30,310 --> 00:03:36,060
It shows you're attuned to external factors affecting everyone, and it positions you as considerate and aware.

71
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:37,520
Okay, good points.

72
00:03:38,220 --> 00:03:40,080
Now let's talk about getting straight to business.

73
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:46,430
For clarity, what about something like, I am writing to follow up on our discussion from last Tuesday's meeting?

74
00:03:46,760 --> 00:03:49,770
Direct, clear, immediately grounds the conversation.

75
00:03:49,790 --> 00:03:51,750
Yeah, no guessing games there.

76
00:03:52,130 --> 00:03:53,890
And it's essential for continuity.

77
00:03:54,690 --> 00:03:59,920
It significantly reduces the recipient's cognitive load because it reminds them of the previous interaction.

78
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:01,990
They don't have to search their memory or their inbox.

79
00:04:02,150 --> 00:04:03,150
Right, saves time.

80
00:04:03,310 --> 00:04:04,190
What about introductions?

81
00:04:04,330 --> 00:04:12,160
Okay, for new introductions, a phrase like, I would like to take this opportunity to introduce myself as the new project manager leaves no room for doubt.

82
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:16,150
Simple, professional, instantly establishes your role.

83
00:04:16,190 --> 00:04:19,200
Clarity is paramount in introductions, isn't it?

84
00:04:19,220 --> 00:04:26,140
This phrase immediately positions you and explains the reasons for contact, crucial for new collaborations or even internal transitions.

85
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:27,200
Makes sense.

86
00:04:27,230 --> 00:04:28,870
And if you're emailing for someone else.

87
00:04:29,530 --> 00:04:29,850
Good one.

88
00:04:30,510 --> 00:04:37,740
Then something like, I am contacting you on behalf of Mr. Johnson concerning our upcoming partnership, adds that layer of authority.

89
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:38,520
That's smart.

90
00:04:38,700 --> 00:04:43,840
It demonstrates legitimacy and a clear chain of communication, lends credibility.

91
00:04:44,160 --> 00:04:44,990
Spot on.

92
00:04:45,170 --> 00:04:46,650
So you've nailed the opening.

93
00:04:46,690 --> 00:04:48,150
Great first impression.

94
00:04:48,190 --> 00:04:55,100
But that only gets you so far if your core message, your request gets lost or isn't clear, right?

95
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:55,900
Absolutely.

96
00:04:56,120 --> 00:05:04,580
So how do you ask for what you need clearly, respectfully and crucially in a way that makes it easy for the other person to say yes and hopefully say it quickly?

97
00:05:04,610 --> 00:05:05,910
That's the real challenge, isn't it?

98
00:05:06,290 --> 00:05:09,920
Well-phrased requests are all about reducing ambiguity and speeding things up.

99
00:05:09,940 --> 00:05:10,220
Okay.

100
00:05:10,460 --> 00:05:17,900
So for seeking information, a phrase like, I am writing to request additional information about your services is wonderfully direct.

101
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:19,110
Straightforward, no fluff.

102
00:05:19,410 --> 00:05:20,710
Tells them exactly what you need from them.

103
00:05:20,860 --> 00:05:21,420
Exactly.

104
00:05:21,780 --> 00:05:23,680
Keeps the focus squarely on their offering.

105
00:05:24,470 --> 00:05:27,510
But when you need specifics, specificity is your superpower.

106
00:05:27,870 --> 00:05:33,170
For example, could you please provide me with an update on the status of our recent order, 45,678?

107
00:05:33,250 --> 00:05:34,630
Ah, yes.

108
00:05:34,790 --> 00:05:37,460
That order number is key.

109
00:05:37,700 --> 00:05:37,920
Yeah.

110
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,080
Cuts out so much potential back and forth.

111
00:05:40,060 --> 00:05:40,520
Totally.

112
00:05:40,880 --> 00:05:44,630
Including that unique identifier makes it incredibly easy for them to find the info.

113
00:05:45,330 --> 00:05:48,190
Avoids those frustrating clarification emails.

114
00:05:48,210 --> 00:05:48,700
Definitely.

115
00:05:49,260 --> 00:05:51,560
What about asking for more detail on something?

116
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:57,970
Okay, another phrase for a detailed inquiry is, I would appreciate it if you could provide more details about your product specifications.

117
00:05:58,630 --> 00:06:02,040
Polite, but also firm in the request for that granular detail.

118
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:03,640
Signals you're serious.

119
00:06:03,690 --> 00:06:08,810
Indeed, it signals a deeper level of interest and helps you qualify the product against your needs.

120
00:06:09,050 --> 00:06:11,190
And for project timelines, always tricky.

121
00:06:11,480 --> 00:06:12,150
Right.

122
00:06:12,420 --> 00:06:16,740
Could you kindly clarify the timeline for the implementation phase of this project?

123
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:21,730
The kindly adds that extra layer of politeness to a direct question, which I think can be helpful.

124
00:06:21,880 --> 00:06:22,230
It does.

125
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:26,850
And precision here is just essential for project management and setting expectations.

126
00:06:26,890 --> 00:06:27,870
Make sure everyone's aligned.

127
00:06:28,330 --> 00:06:28,530
Okay.

128
00:06:28,550 --> 00:06:28,950
Makes sense.

129
00:06:29,390 --> 00:06:31,630
Now, what about action-oriented requests?

130
00:06:32,050 --> 00:06:33,340
Especially with deadlines.

131
00:06:33,380 --> 00:06:33,600
Right.

132
00:06:34,120 --> 00:06:40,500
If you have a deadline, use something like, could you please review the attached document and provide your feedback by Friday?

133
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:42,900
Clear task, clear deadline.

134
00:06:43,060 --> 00:06:49,590
Yeah, and beyond just setting expectations, that by Friday kind of leverages the endowment effect.

135
00:06:49,890 --> 00:06:52,410
It almost gives the recipient ownership of the deadline.

136
00:06:52,430 --> 00:06:54,030
Oh, that's a fascinating insight.

137
00:06:54,740 --> 00:06:57,180
It makes it feel like their deadline, not just yours.

138
00:06:57,420 --> 00:06:57,940
Precisely.

139
00:06:58,100 --> 00:07:00,420
The key insight is always be specific.

140
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:04,710
You know, soon or shortly just leaves too much room for misinterpretation.

141
00:07:04,690 --> 00:07:05,290
Good point.

142
00:07:05,410 --> 00:07:07,270
What if you want a softer approach?

143
00:07:07,290 --> 00:07:14,480
Okay, you might say, would it be possible for you to share the meeting minutes with all participants?

144
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:14,680
Ah, okay.

145
00:07:14,700 --> 00:07:18,370
It offers a polite alternative rather than just demanding it, which I appreciate.

146
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:19,610
Feels more collaborative.

147
00:07:19,750 --> 00:07:20,250
Exactly.

148
00:07:20,830 --> 00:07:26,770
This open-ended phrasing is softer, implies consideration for their workload while still making the request clear.

149
00:07:26,970 --> 00:07:30,060
And if something's really urgent... Then you need to explain why.

150
00:07:31,120 --> 00:07:31,460
Try.

151
00:07:31,500 --> 00:07:36,810
I'd like to ask if you could prioritize this task as we need the results for tomorrow's meeting.

152
00:07:37,670 --> 00:07:37,910
Right.

153
00:07:38,390 --> 00:07:41,440
Providing the why makes the request so much more understandable.

154
00:07:41,460 --> 00:07:43,960
Increases the likelihood they'll cooperate, I bet.

155
00:07:44,110 --> 00:07:44,610
Absolutely.

156
00:07:44,830 --> 00:07:50,880
Explaining the rationale behind a priority request definitely increases cooperation and helps them see the broader impact.

157
00:07:51,010 --> 00:07:59,260
Okay, so beyond just asking for things, a massive part of effective business communication is proactively keeping people informed, right?

158
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:00,760
Even when they haven't asked.

159
00:08:00,780 --> 00:08:01,480
Oh, absolutely.

160
00:08:01,920 --> 00:08:04,770
It's all about building trust and keeping projects running smoothly.

161
00:08:04,890 --> 00:08:05,990
So how do we do that well?

162
00:08:06,290 --> 00:08:10,030
Well, it's all about managing expectations and minimizing uncertainty.

163
00:08:10,730 --> 00:08:13,480
Proactive updates show competence and respect for others' time.

164
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:14,180
Makes sense.

165
00:08:14,400 --> 00:08:15,940
What about confirmations?

166
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:16,440
Okay.

167
00:08:16,460 --> 00:08:23,100
For confirmations, a phrase like, as requested, I have attached the report with the quarterly sales figures is direct and efficient.

168
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:26,790
Fulfills the request, specifies the content, no ambiguity.

169
00:08:26,950 --> 00:08:27,310
Correct.

170
00:08:27,970 --> 00:08:31,520
It confirms you took the action and provides the promised info.

171
00:08:31,540 --> 00:08:32,540
Crucial for record keeping.

172
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:33,260
Next steps.

173
00:08:33,640 --> 00:08:33,920
Okay.

174
00:08:34,830 --> 00:08:36,310
What about delivering good news?

175
00:08:36,740 --> 00:08:38,490
Ah, everyone loves those.

176
00:08:38,910 --> 00:08:44,730
Something like, I'm pleased to confirm that your application has been approved and processing will begin immediately.

177
00:08:44,870 --> 00:08:47,160
That's the kind of email everyone loves to open.

178
00:08:47,180 --> 00:08:51,300
It really underscores how powerful, clear, positive framing can be.

179
00:08:51,540 --> 00:08:51,980
Indeed.

180
00:08:52,540 --> 00:08:58,850
This phrase delivers positive news clearly, sets expectations for immediate action, provides reassurance.

181
00:08:58,870 --> 00:08:59,290
Nice.

182
00:08:59,310 --> 00:09:02,180
And for more formal stuff, like just acknowledging receipt.

183
00:09:02,210 --> 00:09:02,510
Right.

184
00:09:02,690 --> 00:09:08,660
For formal acknowledgement use, I confirm receipt of your email dated May 5th regarding the contract renewal.

185
00:09:08,960 --> 00:09:12,760
Simple, formal, and vital for official correspondence, I imagine.

186
00:09:12,880 --> 00:09:16,610
Essential for formal communication, especially anything legal or contractual.

187
00:09:16,650 --> 00:09:19,810
It gives you an audit trail, ensures both parties are on the same page.

188
00:09:20,030 --> 00:09:21,020
Good point.

189
00:09:21,040 --> 00:09:22,580
Okay, what about project updates?

190
00:09:22,820 --> 00:09:27,090
You can say, I am pleased to inform you that the project is progressing according to schedule.

191
00:09:27,340 --> 00:09:33,330
A positive status report like that builds confidence, probably reduces those constant checking in emails.

192
00:09:33,370 --> 00:09:33,890
Precisely.

193
00:09:34,510 --> 00:09:36,390
Fosters a sense of control and stability.

194
00:09:36,970 --> 00:09:40,400
But if there are changes... You need to be super clear.

195
00:09:40,820 --> 00:09:41,300
Exactly.

196
00:09:41,620 --> 00:09:44,490
For crucial changes, it's imperative to be explicit.

197
00:09:45,150 --> 00:09:48,960
I'm ready to update you that the launch date has been moved forward to May 15th.

198
00:09:48,930 --> 00:09:53,600
Yeah, changes in timelines need absolute clarity to avoid confusion down the line.

199
00:09:53,640 --> 00:09:53,980
They do.

200
00:09:54,020 --> 00:09:58,120
This phrase is clear about the new date and implies proactive management.

201
00:09:58,350 --> 00:10:00,830
And for just keeping internal teams in the loop.

202
00:10:00,850 --> 00:10:08,740
For that, a phrase like, just to keep you in the loop, we've scheduled the client presentation for next Wednesday at 10 a.m. works beautifully.

203
00:10:09,020 --> 00:10:14,790
Casual, but so effective for keeping everyone connected without needing a formal meeting or announcement.

204
00:10:15,070 --> 00:10:17,380
It's perfect for informal but important updates.

205
00:10:17,860 --> 00:10:19,960
Ensures everyone has the necessary context.

206
00:10:20,460 --> 00:10:21,820
Okay, let's shift gears a bit.

207
00:10:22,300 --> 00:10:25,400
Sometimes, despite our best efforts, things go wrong.

208
00:10:25,450 --> 00:10:29,240
Or maybe we need to deliver feedback that isn't entirely positive.

209
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:31,960
This is where email truly gets tricky, right?

210
00:10:32,140 --> 00:10:32,760
It really does.

211
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:38,400
But the right phrases can make all the difference between a minor hiccup and, well, a damaged relationship.

212
00:10:38,420 --> 00:10:48,330
And if we connect this to the bigger picture, these phrases are fundamentally about preserving relationships and demonstrating accountability, vital for long-term business success.

213
00:10:49,130 --> 00:10:50,720
So let's talk apologies.

214
00:10:51,420 --> 00:10:52,980
How do you do that professionally?

215
00:10:53,400 --> 00:11:00,970
Okay, when expressing apologies, you could say something like, I apologize for the delayed response due to my recent business trip.

216
00:11:00,990 --> 00:11:01,170
Right.

217
00:11:01,590 --> 00:11:07,110
Acknowledges the delay, gives a brief professional reason, no oversharing, no excuses.

218
00:11:07,190 --> 00:11:07,720
Exactly.

219
00:11:08,290 --> 00:11:11,840
Shows respect for their time without bogging them down in unnecessary details.

220
00:11:12,400 --> 00:11:13,680
For a more formal apology.

221
00:11:13,760 --> 00:11:15,220
What would that sound like?

222
00:11:15,240 --> 00:11:20,130
Consider, please accept our sincere apologies for the inconvenience this may have caused.

223
00:11:20,550 --> 00:11:21,730
Formal and empathetic.

224
00:11:21,770 --> 00:11:22,570
Hits the right notes.

225
00:11:22,630 --> 00:11:24,110
Seems like a robust apology.

226
00:11:24,270 --> 00:11:24,530
It is.

227
00:11:24,890 --> 00:11:30,980
Takes responsibility, acknowledges the impact on the recipient, crucial for customer service, maintaining goodwill.

228
00:11:31,160 --> 00:11:33,740
Okay, but here's where it gets really strategic, I think.

229
00:11:33,760 --> 00:11:36,210
What about combining an apology with a solution?

230
00:11:36,830 --> 00:11:37,270
Ah, yes.

231
00:11:38,070 --> 00:11:38,650
Very powerful.

232
00:11:39,090 --> 00:11:44,290
For example, we take full responsibility for the shipping delay and are offering a discount on your next order.

233
00:11:44,340 --> 00:11:49,920
See, not just an apology, but a fix that shifts from regret to action.

234
00:11:49,980 --> 00:11:52,200
So effective in restoring trust.

235
00:11:52,520 --> 00:11:53,000
Absolutely.

236
00:11:53,280 --> 00:11:59,500
That combo of accountability with a tangible resolution is a powerful way to mitigate dissatisfaction.

237
00:11:59,480 --> 00:12:00,100
Definitely.

238
00:12:00,640 --> 00:12:04,030
Now, what about responding to issues or concerns they raise?

239
00:12:04,190 --> 00:12:04,750
Okay, try.

240
00:12:04,850 --> 00:12:09,550
I understand your concerns regarding the project timeline and would like to discuss possible solutions.

241
00:12:09,710 --> 00:12:10,200
I like that.

242
00:12:10,580 --> 00:12:14,060
It validates their feelings first, then immediately pivots to problem solving.

243
00:12:14,460 --> 00:12:16,900
That feels like a masterclass in de-escalation.

244
00:12:17,100 --> 00:12:17,470
Indeed.

245
00:12:18,150 --> 00:12:20,110
Highly effective way to move towards a resolution.

246
00:12:20,730 --> 00:12:24,210
Another key phrase is, thank you for bringing this issue to our attention.

247
00:12:24,490 --> 00:12:26,520
We take such matters very seriously.

248
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:31,380
Makes the sender feel heard, assures them their feedback is valued, not dismissed.

249
00:12:31,560 --> 00:12:32,000
Exactly.

250
00:12:32,430 --> 00:12:34,970
Conveys professionalism and a commitment to quality.

251
00:12:35,610 --> 00:12:40,620
Similarly, your feedback about our customer service experience is valuable and will help us improve.

252
00:12:40,920 --> 00:12:41,880
Oh, that's brilliant.

253
00:12:42,380 --> 00:12:45,130
It transforms criticism into constructive input.

254
00:12:45,230 --> 00:12:45,670
Precisely.

255
00:12:46,350 --> 00:12:48,450
Turns a critique into an opportunity for growth.

256
00:12:49,110 --> 00:12:51,360
Signals an organization that's open to learning.

257
00:12:51,520 --> 00:12:52,490
Love that framing.

258
00:12:53,150 --> 00:12:54,610
OK, what about giving feedback?

259
00:12:54,950 --> 00:12:57,720
Constructive criticism via email can be tough.

260
00:12:57,740 --> 00:12:58,060
It can.

261
00:12:58,980 --> 00:13:01,130
A good approach is the sandwich method, perhaps.

262
00:13:01,850 --> 00:13:03,030
Start positive.

263
00:13:03,550 --> 00:13:08,300
The report was comprehensive, but it might benefit from a more concise executive summary.

264
00:13:08,520 --> 00:13:08,720
Right.

265
00:13:09,090 --> 00:13:15,200
Positive reinforcement first, then a gentle, actionable suggestion makes feedback much easier to absorb.

266
00:13:15,440 --> 00:13:15,820
It does.

267
00:13:16,520 --> 00:13:18,760
And always remember to highlight what went well, too.

268
00:13:19,590 --> 00:13:20,650
Specific praise.

269
00:13:21,270 --> 00:13:26,020
Your team has made significant progress, and I especially noticed the improvements in response time.

270
00:13:26,170 --> 00:13:32,100
Yeah, specific praise like that makes feedback much more impactful and motivational than just general compliments, right?

271
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:32,600
Definitely.

272
00:13:33,160 --> 00:13:37,860
Highlighting specific positive developments reinforces good performance and encourages more of it.

273
00:13:38,210 --> 00:13:40,690
Okay, email isn't just for reporting or reacting, is it?

274
00:13:40,990 --> 00:13:46,140
It's also a powerful tool for proactively shaping your schedule and exploring new opportunities.

275
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:46,800
Absolutely.

276
00:13:47,200 --> 00:13:51,460
It's all about making it easy for others to say yes to your proposals, whatever they might be.

277
00:13:52,210 --> 00:13:53,730
So let's talk scheduling meetings.

278
00:13:53,950 --> 00:13:55,330
How do you do that effectively?

279
00:13:55,480 --> 00:13:57,140
Well, a direct approach works well.

280
00:13:57,980 --> 00:14:02,090
Could we schedule a meeting next Tuesday at 2 p.m. to discuss the proposal?

281
00:14:02,790 --> 00:14:03,830
Specific suggestion.

282
00:14:04,550 --> 00:14:08,150
Makes it efficient for them to just confirm or propose an alternative.

283
00:14:08,670 --> 00:14:10,250
Saves that back-and-forth dance.

284
00:14:10,270 --> 00:14:10,720
Exactly.

285
00:14:10,920 --> 00:14:11,920
No starting from scratch.

286
00:14:11,980 --> 00:14:15,040
Giving a concrete time and topic saves so much hassle.

287
00:14:15,160 --> 00:14:16,960
What about offering more flexibility?

288
00:14:17,260 --> 00:14:18,610
Okay, you could offer a window.

289
00:14:19,210 --> 00:14:23,190
Are you available for a 30-minute call tomorrow between 10 a.m. and noon?

290
00:14:23,470 --> 00:14:27,780
That shows consideration for their schedule while still defining the scope, the length of the call.

291
00:14:27,900 --> 00:14:30,580
Right, and for virtual meetings, thoroughness is key.

292
00:14:30,620 --> 00:14:31,640
You really need to spell it out.

293
00:14:32,260 --> 00:14:36,690
Would it be convenient for you to meet via Zoom next Monday at 9 a.m. Eastern time?

294
00:14:37,390 --> 00:14:41,650
Ah, specifying the platform and the time zone, absolutely crucial for remote teams.

295
00:14:41,970 --> 00:14:43,500
Prevents those logistical headaches.

296
00:14:43,700 --> 00:14:45,920
Essential for virtual meetings, definitely.

297
00:14:46,620 --> 00:14:50,060
Now, let's pivot slightly to proactive sales and marketing outreach.

298
00:14:50,420 --> 00:14:52,160
Okay, how do you grab attention there?

299
00:14:52,310 --> 00:14:54,510
You might open with a strong value proposition.

300
00:14:55,340 --> 00:14:59,980
I am writing to introduce our new service that could potentially reduce your operating costs by 20%.

301
00:15:00,100 --> 00:15:00,900
Right.

302
00:15:01,080 --> 00:15:03,750
Straight up front, you immediately tell them what's in it for them.

303
00:15:04,350 --> 00:15:06,170
Addresses a potential pain point.

304
00:15:06,190 --> 00:15:06,650
Exactly.

305
00:15:07,010 --> 00:15:09,110
Leads with a clear benefit, captures attention.

306
00:15:09,710 --> 00:15:11,670
Then, for a clear call to action.

307
00:15:11,770 --> 00:15:12,510
What's a good phrase?

308
00:15:12,790 --> 00:15:19,180
Consider, would you be interested in scheduling a demo to see how our software could streamline your workflow?

309
00:15:19,520 --> 00:15:22,020
Focuses on their benefit, how it could help them.

310
00:15:22,450 --> 00:15:24,410
And it's a low commitment next step.

311
00:15:24,430 --> 00:15:24,970
Precisely.

312
00:15:25,410 --> 00:15:28,520
Moves directly from the potential benefit to an easy action.

313
00:15:29,150 --> 00:15:31,530
Makes it simple for the prospect to explore further.

314
00:15:31,630 --> 00:15:34,150
And for existing customers or relationships.

315
00:15:34,740 --> 00:15:36,360
Ah, leveraging that relationship is smart.

316
00:15:36,680 --> 00:15:43,410
A phrase like, as a valued customer, we would like to offer you exclusive early access to our premium subscription can be very effective.

317
00:15:43,630 --> 00:15:46,750
Yeah, it creates that sense of exclusivity and appreciation.

318
00:15:47,350 --> 00:15:48,390
Appeals the loyalty.

319
00:15:48,530 --> 00:15:48,830
It does.

320
00:15:49,430 --> 00:15:50,560
Provides a perceived privilege.

321
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:52,660
Highly effective in retaining customers.

322
00:15:53,060 --> 00:15:54,500
Maybe encouraging upgrades.

323
00:15:54,480 --> 00:15:55,880
Okay, let's talk about nudges.

324
00:15:56,380 --> 00:16:00,110
Sometimes we need to remind people without sounding like we're nagging, right?

325
00:16:00,790 --> 00:16:03,870
Or maybe clearly assign tasks without creating confusion.

326
00:16:04,150 --> 00:16:06,340
It's definitely a delicate balance.

327
00:16:06,360 --> 00:16:06,620
It is.

328
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:15,150
And what's powerful here is how these phrases enable clear communication and efficient workflow without damaging those professional relationships.

329
00:16:15,170 --> 00:16:18,410
So how do you send effective, polite reminders?

330
00:16:18,510 --> 00:16:25,340
Okay, a good starting point is, this is a friendly reminder that the deadline for submitting your report is this Friday, May 12th.

331
00:16:25,660 --> 00:16:31,330
Right, the word friendly softens it, while the specific deadline keeps it clear and actionable.

332
00:16:31,890 --> 00:16:33,280
Gentle yet firm.

333
00:16:33,420 --> 00:16:33,940
Exactly.

334
00:16:34,600 --> 00:16:43,350
For an even softer touch, maybe for someone who's usually reliable, you might say, just a gentle reminder that we need your input on the proposal by the end of this week.

335
00:16:43,540 --> 00:16:44,220
Even softer.

336
00:16:44,720 --> 00:16:45,480
Less demanding.

337
00:16:46,200 --> 00:16:49,840
Good for maintaining that collaborative vibe while still prompting action.

338
00:16:50,180 --> 00:16:50,700
Precisely.

339
00:16:51,060 --> 00:16:53,310
And if you need a more direct follow-up on something overdue.

340
00:16:53,590 --> 00:16:53,930
What then?

341
00:16:54,150 --> 00:16:54,370
Try.

342
00:16:54,410 --> 00:16:58,650
I'm following up on my previous email regarding the outstanding invoice due last month.

343
00:16:58,830 --> 00:16:59,230
Direct.

344
00:16:59,510 --> 00:16:59,790
Yes.

345
00:17:00,050 --> 00:17:03,840
But referring back to the previous email indicates persistence without aggression.

346
00:17:04,020 --> 00:17:05,160
It shows you've asked before.

347
00:17:05,220 --> 00:17:05,520
Right.

348
00:17:06,060 --> 00:17:12,240
It grounds the current email in a history of communication, making it less abrupt than a brand new reminder out of the blue.

349
00:17:12,310 --> 00:17:12,850
Makes sense.

350
00:17:13,690 --> 00:17:14,930
Okay, switching to delegation.

351
00:17:15,290 --> 00:17:17,070
How do you assign tasks with clarity?

352
00:17:17,550 --> 00:17:18,170
Be specific.

353
00:17:19,040 --> 00:17:19,340
Try.

354
00:17:19,940 --> 00:17:23,980
Could you please prepare a summary report of the quarterly results by next Wednesday?

355
00:17:24,520 --> 00:17:28,120
Specific task, specific output, specific deadline.

356
00:17:28,590 --> 00:17:30,010
No room for ambiguity there.

357
00:17:30,190 --> 00:17:32,490
That's the cornerstone of effective delegation, isn't it?

358
00:17:32,670 --> 00:17:33,150
Absolutely.

359
00:17:33,750 --> 00:17:40,360
For a more formal assignment, you could use, I'd like to assign you responsibility for coordinating the team's input on this project.

360
00:17:40,440 --> 00:17:44,630
Okay, that's a very clear articulation of a specific role and responsibility.

361
00:17:44,810 --> 00:17:47,830
Crucial for project clarity and individual accountability.

362
00:17:48,750 --> 00:17:52,620
And finally, for a polite request that includes a follow-up action.

363
00:17:52,920 --> 00:17:53,840
Like taking minutes.

364
00:17:54,120 --> 00:17:54,600
Exactly.

365
00:17:54,720 --> 00:17:59,390
Consider, would you mind taking meeting minutes during tomorrow's client call and distributing them afterward?

366
00:17:59,660 --> 00:18:04,730
The would you mind softens it, but the actions taking minutes distributing are very explicit.

367
00:18:04,810 --> 00:18:07,110
Ensures the whole task is understood and completed.

368
00:18:07,150 --> 00:18:07,810
Spot on.

369
00:18:08,650 --> 00:18:09,900
Okay, now for the really tricky bit.

370
00:18:10,640 --> 00:18:15,240
Nobody likes delivering bad news, but in business it's, well, it's inevitable sometimes.

371
00:18:15,420 --> 00:18:16,490
Unfortunately, yes.

372
00:18:16,470 --> 00:18:23,930
So how do you say no while maintaining respect, professionalism, and ideally leaving the door open for future opportunities?

373
00:18:24,190 --> 00:18:26,650
This is where the right phrases are absolutely crucial.

374
00:18:26,790 --> 00:18:32,370
And if we connect this to the bigger picture, a well-phrased decline can genuinely prevent burning bridges.

375
00:18:32,750 --> 00:18:35,420
Okay, so rejecting proposals gracefully.

376
00:18:35,460 --> 00:18:36,460
How do we do that?

377
00:18:36,740 --> 00:18:43,670
A phrase like, after careful consideration, we regret to inform you that we cannot proceed with your proposal at this time.

378
00:18:43,800 --> 00:18:44,140
Right.

379
00:18:44,340 --> 00:18:50,630
It acknowledges thought went into it, delivers the clear negative, but keeps future possibilities open with, at this time.

380
00:18:50,670 --> 00:18:52,410
Firm, but polite.

381
00:18:52,390 --> 00:18:53,020
Exactly.

382
00:18:53,230 --> 00:18:54,980
Implies due diligence, avoids abruptness.

383
00:18:55,640 --> 00:19:03,110
For job applications, a common one is, thank you for your application, but we have decided to pursue other candidates whose qualifications better match our needs.

384
00:19:03,420 --> 00:19:08,850
Expresses gratitude, offers a brief, objective reason, doesn't get overly personal or critical.

385
00:19:09,150 --> 00:19:09,330
Right.

386
00:19:09,650 --> 00:19:15,600
Providing a general, non-personal reason helps the recipient understand without feeling personally attacked.

387
00:19:15,780 --> 00:19:16,620
Okay.

388
00:19:16,640 --> 00:19:19,700
What about declining a specific request, like a discount?

389
00:19:19,750 --> 00:19:25,740
A good phrase is, while we appreciate your interest, we are unable to accommodate your request for a discount.

390
00:19:26,780 --> 00:19:29,750
Ah, validates their interest first before delivering the decline.

391
00:19:29,970 --> 00:19:31,530
That always softens the blow, I think.

392
00:19:31,850 --> 00:19:32,170
It does.

393
00:19:32,530 --> 00:19:36,920
Acknowledging their request first shows respect before stating the limitation.

394
00:19:37,300 --> 00:19:40,490
And for proposals that are good but just don't fit right now...

395
00:19:40,870 --> 00:19:41,670
Try this.

396
00:19:42,170 --> 00:19:47,200
Though your proposal has many merits, it falls outside the scope of our current strategic objectives.

397
00:19:47,220 --> 00:19:48,880
Oh, that's an excellent approach.

398
00:19:48,900 --> 00:19:57,910
Positive reinforcement before the bad news and an objective strategic reason for the decline frames it as a mismatch, not a judgment of their work.

399
00:19:57,930 --> 00:19:58,600
Very smart.

400
00:19:58,620 --> 00:19:59,500
It can be very effective.

401
00:19:59,840 --> 00:20:00,720
Okay, we've covered a lot.

402
00:20:01,240 --> 00:20:05,370
From mastering the art of the perfect hello to gracefully delivering tough news.

403
00:20:05,780 --> 00:20:07,200
But how do you tie it all together?

404
00:20:07,550 --> 00:20:12,550
What's the best way to sign off and ensure your message lands perfectly, leaving the right final impression?

405
00:20:12,860 --> 00:20:17,740
Yeah, the closing is often where you reinforce the call to action or express anticipation.

406
00:20:18,240 --> 00:20:19,610
It guides the next steps.

407
00:20:20,070 --> 00:20:22,350
So for prompting a response, what's a go-to?

408
00:20:22,650 --> 00:20:27,000
Well, a classic is, I look forward to hearing from you at your earliest convenience.

409
00:20:27,300 --> 00:20:32,870
Classic, polite, definitely encourages a timely response without sounding demanding.

410
00:20:33,210 --> 00:20:35,150
It's universally accepted, professional.

411
00:20:35,870 --> 00:20:37,430
Signals you expect to reply.

412
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:38,560
To offer help.

413
00:20:38,920 --> 00:20:39,480
What's good there?

414
00:20:40,300 --> 00:20:40,480
Use.

415
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,480
Please don't hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

416
00:20:43,630 --> 00:20:44,320
Offers help.

417
00:20:44,490 --> 00:20:45,640
Invites further dialogue.

418
00:20:46,160 --> 00:20:48,800
Removes barriers for clarification, which is useful.

419
00:20:49,040 --> 00:20:50,780
Especially good for complex subjects.

420
00:20:51,480 --> 00:20:54,230
Now, for setting clear expectations about future contact.

421
00:20:54,330 --> 00:20:55,090
How do you phrase that?

422
00:20:55,410 --> 00:20:59,990
You can say, I will follow up next week to discuss the next steps in more detail.

423
00:21:00,020 --> 00:21:03,080
Provides a transparent roadmap for what comes next.

424
00:21:03,540 --> 00:21:04,700
Reduces uncertainty.

425
00:21:05,060 --> 00:21:05,780
Shows you're committed.

426
00:21:06,040 --> 00:21:06,540
Exactly.

427
00:21:06,580 --> 00:21:07,770
It shows commitment to the issue.

428
00:21:09,010 --> 00:21:10,470
To express appreciation.

429
00:21:10,550 --> 00:21:12,130
Like after they've reviewed something.

430
00:21:12,170 --> 00:21:12,370
Right.

431
00:21:12,710 --> 00:21:12,970
Try.

432
00:21:13,250 --> 00:21:16,080
Thank you for your time and consideration in reviewing our proposal.

433
00:21:16,320 --> 00:21:17,380
Professional gratitude.

434
00:21:17,740 --> 00:21:20,800
Especially important when someone has invested their time, like you said.

435
00:21:20,960 --> 00:21:24,900
Fosters good professional relationships, even if the outcome isn't known yet.

436
00:21:25,240 --> 00:21:28,870
And for building relationships, expressing enthusiasm for future work.

437
00:21:29,170 --> 00:21:33,450
You might say, we look forward to the opportunity of working with you on this exciting project.

438
00:21:33,730 --> 00:21:36,540
Projects are really positive outlook for future collaborations.

439
00:21:36,560 --> 00:21:37,540
That's a good tone.

440
00:21:37,990 --> 00:21:46,300
reinforces a desire for partnership, key for long-term business development, and finally, for existing clients or partners.

441
00:21:46,520 --> 00:21:48,270
How do you close strongly there?

442
00:21:48,630 --> 00:21:52,070
A phrase like, thank you for your continued support and trust in our services.

443
00:21:52,610 --> 00:21:57,580
Ah, reinforces loyalty and clearly states how much you value their ongoing business.

444
00:21:57,980 --> 00:21:58,580
Powerful.

445
00:21:58,720 --> 00:21:59,590
It's a strong closing.

446
00:22:00,230 --> 00:22:03,910
Acknowledges their ongoing relationship and reinforces their importance.

447
00:22:03,990 --> 00:22:07,940
So wrapping this up then, what does this all mean for you listening?

448
00:22:08,490 --> 00:22:17,710
From navigating that delicate dance of introductions to delivering tough news with grace, mastering these essential business email phrases isn't just about sounding smart, is it?

449
00:22:17,890 --> 00:22:18,690
No, not at all.

450
00:22:18,830 --> 00:22:24,670
It's about communicating effectively, building stronger relationships, and ultimately achieving your goals.

451
00:22:24,850 --> 00:22:25,390
Indeed.

452
00:22:26,050 --> 00:22:29,400
The precision and the intent behind these phrases.

453
00:22:30,380 --> 00:22:31,420
It's not just etiquette.

454
00:22:31,740 --> 00:22:33,240
It's strategic communication.

455
00:22:33,300 --> 00:22:33,560
Right.

456
00:22:33,580 --> 00:22:39,810
They are powerful tools for clarity, for respect, and for influence in this digital landscape we all operate in.

457
00:22:40,670 --> 00:22:46,880
Choosing the right phrase is a subtle yet really significant investment in your professional credibility.

458
00:22:46,970 --> 00:22:49,660
So as you craft your next email, maybe consider this.

459
00:22:50,260 --> 00:22:51,820
An email isn't just a message.

460
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:53,560
It's like a digital handshake.

461
00:22:54,080 --> 00:22:54,620
Good analogy.

462
00:22:54,770 --> 00:22:59,430
Are you ensuring your handshake is firm, clear, and leaves that lasting positive impression?

463
00:23:00,210 --> 00:23:05,220
What subtle shifts in your email language could redefine your professional presence starting today?

464
00:23:05,360 --> 00:23:06,060
Something to think about.

465
00:23:06,320 --> 00:23:06,860
Definitely.

466
